description:
{
	"name": "Reduction-Synchronized Velocity Bandit (RSVB)",
	"algorithm": "Step 1: Define two restart strategy arms: Arm 0 (Aggressive Luby, focused search) and Arm 1 (Geometric/Exponential, stable search). Step 2: For every conflict, maintain a sliding window of the last 128 backjump distances ($D$) and conflict levels ($L$). Step 3: Calculate 'Search Velocity' ($V$) as the ratio of the average backjump distance to the average conflict level ($V = \text{avg}(D) / \text{avg}(L)$). Step 4: Define the Reward ($R$) for the active arm as $R = V \cdot (1 - \text{LBD}_{\text{avg}} / 64)$, capping the LBD influence to avoid rewarding high-LBD local search. Step 5: Update the arm selection using a modified UCB1 formula where the exploration constant ($C$) is non-static: $C_t = 0.8 \cdot \sigma(V)$, where $\sigma(V)$ is the standard deviation of velocity over the sliding window. Step 6: If $V$ falls below a threshold of 0.02 for more than 512 consecutive conflicts, trigger an 'Epsilon-Greedy Reset' where the bandit ignores previous rewards for 100 restarts to force exploration of the alternate arm. Step 7: Synchronize reward decay with the solver's clause reduction phases by applying a dynamic decay factor $\gamma = 0.95 - (0.1 \cdot \sigma(V))$ every time a 'reduce' operation occurs, ensuring the bandit's memory is purged more aggressively when the solver discards learned information and search instability is high.",
	"reason": "By replacing the fixed conflict-based interval in Step 7 with a reduction-synchronized decay, the bandit better aligns its learning cycles with the solver's internal state transitions. Using a dynamic decay factor tied to velocity variance $(\sigma(V))$ allows the solver to forget outdated arm performance more quickly during volatile search phases, facilitating faster adaptation to the new clause database structure post-reduction."
}

